{
  "title": "Changed hub and functional connectivity patterns of the posterior fusiform gyrus in chess experts",
  "data": {
    "title": "Changed hub and functional connectivity patterns of the posterior fusiform gyrus in chess experts",
    "text": "Whole brain voxel-wise functional connectivity strength (FCS) calculation\nThe voxel-wise FCS value was calculated for each subject.\nSpecifically, for each voxel, the FCS was calculated as the\naverage of the correlations between this voxel and all other\nvoxels in the brain. First, the functional connectivity calcula-\ntion was constrained within a binary gray matter mask created\nby thresholding the gray matter probability template with 0.2\n(Liu et al. 2018; Wang et al. 2014; Wu et al. 2016a). The time\nseries of the seed voxel and all other voxels in the rest of brain\nwere extracted, and Pearson’s correlation coefficients between\nthe time series of one seed voxel and that of all other voxels\nwere calculated. Then, a threshold of 0.25 was set to remove\nweak connections that may arise from signal noise (Liu et al.\n2018; Wang et al. 2014; Wu et al. 2016a). Next, the FCS was\ncalculated over the whole brain by averaging the correlation\ncoefficients that were higher than this threshold. We repeated\nthe entire process for all other voxels, and an FCS map for\neach subject was obtained. Subsequently, the FCS map was\ntransformed to Z scores to improve normality. Such an FCS\nmetric is referred to as the “degree centrality” of weighted\nnetworks in terms of graph theory (Buckner et al. 2009; Zuo\net al. 2012).\n\nFunctional connectivity analyses\nTo\ncalculate the functional connectivity, the mean time se-\nries of the identified hub regions were first extracted.\nThen, the strength of the functional connectivity was\nmeasured by means of Pearson’s correlations between\nthe averaged time series of the hub regions and the\nvoxels of the rest brain. Subsequently, the Fisher’s Z\ntransformation was applied to improve the normality of\nthe original correlation maps."
  },
